<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avidius Cassius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 130–175</span> · Also known as Gaius Avidius Cassius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Avidius Cassius was a Roman general born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He commanded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a>'s Parthian war, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> in 165, and was later given authority over the eastern provinces. In 175, on a false report of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>'s death, he proclaimed himself emperor with support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Syria, but he was killed by his own officers after about three months.</p></div>
